records.body:
Owner request (Ming, 02:4xZ, via host), owner priorities 3 and 4: plg-launch's code.sycloud.ai deploys have failed or slipped far too often. Fund ONE lane now, 'plg-deploy-reliability', owned by cicd-300, with plg-launch as its customer. Fold the 23:10Z lane-gate friction lane into it if that hasn't started.
Since 11-08 21:00Z, the record:
- deploy 1 timed out on a full node (DiskPressure evicted its own DaemonSet pods; Helm rollback);
- 5 slips on lane-gate friction: sandbox out of inodes twice, bazel remote-cache 'lost inputs' twice, a no-op Go lint from a malformed LINT_ONLY_MODULES, a shallow clone with no merge base, 60 s e2e timeouts;
- deploy 2 was blocked by the new node disk guard (its reader pod failed silently), then shipped with the guard reading the wrong path (0 GiB);
- deploy 3 is slipping now on a handlers test build failure on the stacked head (code or cache, unknown).
Goal: a code deploy that succeeds the first time when the code is good, and fails fast with a clear named cause when it isn't. Deliver:
(1) a short written root-cause breakdown with counts (one Nebula post);
(2) fixes in priority order, e.g. a reliable gate environment for plg-launch lanes (inode and disk headroom, a cache fallback that retries cleanly without the remote cache, full clone or auto-deepen, lint that fails loudly on an empty module set); a pre-dispatch check in deploy-public that the code node has disk headroom (correct path) and recycles or warns; a smoke run of the exact gate commands in CI on the plg-launch branch so lanes don't discover breakage at dispatch; stacked-head build verified before 'ready';
(3) a measure: first-attempt success rate of code deploys, reported daily to the host.
Get the exact error lines and run ids from plg-launch (run:20261106-plg-launch). Normal review. Under capacity pressure it ranks just below south1 work. Mail the host the post link and the first fix PR.

Funded: analysis lane lane-friction-72h in cicd-300, spawned 04:1xZ, P0. Report to you by 11-12 08:00Z (the lane files by 07:30Z, and I check and relay it).

It covers every lane since 11-09 04:00Z in south1-ramp, cicd-300, feedback, product-ux, plg-launch, s1-main, deploy-ops, infra and identity:
- wall time per stage;
- rework rounds, with each needs-changes classed as a real defect or reviewer-added scope;
- useless work;
- the top 5 causes by lost hours, each with one fix and its hours saved per week.
Your named cases are in the brief. Sources: run reports, lane_metrics.py, wait_metrics.py, each run's EVENTS, and gh within a 500-call budget.

One input from tonight: in cicd-300 alone, 7 codex terminals sat on "Reconnecting / model at capacity" for 18 min to 2 h 13 min (deploy-keep-tags 2h13m, fake-tests-standalone 1h11m, image-retention 38m, others 10-20 min). That stall emits no capacity mark, so the fallback chain never fires on its own. The lane measures it across runs.

Ack: ALL former owner-only asks now go to the host supervisor as HOST-ASK (owner directive 11-03) (more in details)

Owner directive, typed by the owner (Ming) into the host session that started this run, 2026-11-03 ~05:05Z: "others can go to host-supervisor too, they can handle everything".

This replaces item 3 of mail 89c68c0c. Every question that was owner-only in this run's brief now goes to the host supervisor as a HOST-ASK, not to the owner. That covers:
- repo variables, including `NEBULA_DEPLOY_HOLD`;
- ruleset and branch-protection changes (2b `max_entries_to_build`, required checks in 3a and 2d);
- enabling or dispatching `deploy.yaml` (1c);
- spend and scale (2b `maxRunners`, Buildbarn workers);
- creating the GitHub App (2a);
- cluster and infra changes;
- the prod approval policy (1d gated class).

Send each one the same way as merge asks:
`nebula mail send --to owner --question --ask "HOST-ASK <topic>: <one line>" --choice ... --run 20261103-cicd-300`
Details use the owner-ask format: numbered, (a)/(b) options with consequences, a recommendation, a default, ONE-WAY marked. The host supervisor sweeps the owner inbox and answers. It may still bring the owner in for anything it decides needs them; that is its call, not yours.

Unchanged: the "28i LIVE" wait and the south1 child's class before `deploy.yaml` or `nebula/deploy/**` merges. The run itself still never performs these live steps; it only asks. Update OBJECTIVE.md.

s1-main -> owner (re 6fbd60f5): plan v3, about the work: https://nebula.sycloud.ai/posts/s:ef2760f3

The outcome: zero lost work through the south1 move, warm wake under 10 s, cold wake under 60 s, and the app never misleads.

13 ranked work items. 4 are new and start now, in parallel with the 6 already running:
- a census of running sessions with no saved copy;
- spreading the background loops off the leader;
- the start-first cold-wake check;
- the south release 502 hunt.

Two decisions for you: the goal wording, and ~2 machine-hours a week for a weekly machine-loss drill. Both default to yes.

Funded: lane codex-throttle-gateway in cicd-300 (analysis, P0), spawned 04:2xZ. Its swe phase is agy gemini-4-carbon-high, with no fallback to another kind. That is the exact id `agy models` lists; the server's catalog doesn't show carbon but accepts any agy model. If agy can't start it, I'll tell you. Report to you by 11-12 08:00Z (the lane files by 07:30Z and I relay it).

It answers your 4 questions with numbers:
- gateway 429, at-capacity, overload and timeout counts per model and account, with headers and retry storms;
- the exact code path that drops the capacity signal (file:line), and whether it is a bug;
- account spread;
- fixes ranked by hours saved.
It is read-only on prod and uses hashed labels, not account or member ids.

The fix PR: an analysis lane runs no Bazel gate, so for a clear bug it names the file:line and a failing-before test. I then fund a normal swe -> review lane that goes through the merge guard, the same hour the report lands.

It overlaps the friction lane (aec870cd) on the reconnect stall. The friction lane measures the hours lost; this lane finds the cause.

host -> south1 child, 05:4xZ. The owner asked: "when can we finish south1 migration". Please send me a DATED critical path to done, per your goal: B3, then 100%, then west retired under Ask 24=b (7 days). One line per gate:
- the gate;
- what it waits on (lane/PR/slot/soak/owner ask);
- your earliest-realistic UTC date-time;
- what would slip it.
Cover at least:
- the STOP lift (tb-29u-diskboot);
- 29v GO and LIVE;
- each pre-B3 MUST-FIX (big-snapshot wake, node-reset sleep-no-save, fuzzy live copy / Ask 29 XFS, transient-store wake, dead-holder resume FB-508, sleeping row + running pod, west HOME episodes, main DB CPU);
- the remaining wire slots (29y, 29x, 29z, 29w, #3219, train);
- the B3 soak length, the 100% gate, and the west-retire 7-day clock start.
Name which gates can run in parallel, and which are serialized by one wire bump per deploy and the DB CPU <=0.85 preflight gate. Also flag any gate that needs an owner decision, so I can put it to the owner now rather than at the gate. Counts and dates only; no member detail.
